    "narrative": "For today\u2019s Independence Day slate, **Lawrence Butler** (73.47) and **Tyler Freeman** (71.80) represent our core offensive pillars. **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a high-leverage slot in the order against Alcantara, buoyed by a substantial boost from his dynamic platoon split. Meanwhile, **Tyler Freeman** thrives in a favorable park environment against Ray, and despite a slight drag from an elite opposing catcher's pop time, his efficiency score remains elite. **Luis Arraez** (66.78) also provides a high floor, as the matchup against Sullivan offers a significant park-adjusted boost that mitigates the inherent volatility of his profile.\n\nOur most difficult personnel decision centered on **Taylor Ward**, who sat right on the edge of viability with a 42.03 score. Ultimately, the projection model relegated him to the bench because the matchup against Greene\u2014an elite arm with a low xERA\u2014depressed his ceiling compared to the primary lineup. Conversely, **Bryson Stott** remains a \"Strong\" start at 64.40, though he requires close monitoring; his 40% rain risk is the primary concern for the day, and we have depth like **Brendan Donovan** or **Gleyber Torres** on the bench should the Philadelphia game face significant delays. \n\nWhile **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Will Smith** remain sidelined, our depth continues to be tested by high-skill pitching. **Daulton Varsho** (49.31) and **Gavin Sheets** (45.19) are currently occupying the bottom of our start-worthy range, with the latter facing a tough task against Yamamoto. We are keeping a close eye on **Masyn Winn**, whose efficiency (48.49) is being hampered by the challenge of facing Imanaga. If the weather in Philadelphia turns, we will be forced to pivot, likely shifting **Brendan Donovan** into the mix to ensure we maintain our competitive edge in the 5x5 race, particularly in the ever-vital stolen base category.",
